<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of Mato Grosso</h1><p class="st-meta">1748 CE – 1821 CE · capital Vila Bela da Santíssima Trindade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Brazil's far-western frontier captaincy, born of a gold rush at Cuiabá and built to hold the Guaporé river border against Spain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Paulista bandeirantes led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pascoal-moreira-cabral">Pascoal Moreira Cabral</a> found gold near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuiaba">Cuiabá</a> in 1718, and miners reached it by long canoe convoys called monções from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>. To secure the western frontier with Spanish lands, the crown separated Mato Grosso from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-sao-paulo">Captaincy of São Paulo</a> in 1748. Its first governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-rolim-de-moura">Antônio Rolim de Moura</a>, founded the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vila-bela-da-santissima-trindade">Vila Bela da Santíssima Trindade</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guapore-river">Guaporé</a> river in 1752.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mato Grosso was a remote military frontier facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuit-missions-of-moxos">Moxos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuit-missions-of-chiquitos">Chiquitos</a> in Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a>. Its governors began the massive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-principe-da-beira">Real Forte Príncipe da Beira</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guapore-river">Guaporé</a> in 1776. Gold mining relied on enslaved Africans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vila-bela-da-santissima-trindade">Vila Bela</a> had a largely black population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1750">Treaty of Madrid</a> of 1750 recognised Portuguese possession of much of the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As gold declined, the population fell. Mato Grosso became a province in 1821, and in 1835 its capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuiaba">Cuiabá</a>. Its territory was later divided into the states of Mato Grosso, Mato Grosso do Sul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rondonia">Rondônia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-of-mato-grosso.jpg" alt="Captaincy of Mato Grosso" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: SVG implementation by E2m · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandeira_de_Mato_Grosso.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1748 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vila Bela da Santíssima Trindade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antônio Rolim de Moura</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mato Grosso</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1784&amp;focus=captaincy-of-mato-grosso" aria-label="Captaincy of Mato Grosso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3134 2656 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Captaincy of Mato Grosso lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3454 2912L3944 2917M3454 2912L3718 3265"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3944 2917h0M3718 3265h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3454 2912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3454 2912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1784&amp;focus=captaincy-of-mato-grosso">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
